WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.347 --> 00:00:11.541 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Africa capitals, episode five of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:20.216 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From the middle of the continent we follow the Congo down to the Atlantic, and
then the Zambezi basin to the Indian Ocean.

3
00:00:20.906 --> 00:00:23.124 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bangui into view.

4
00:00:33.763 --> 00:00:39.777 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bangui stands on the Ubangi River in the Central African Republic, in the very
middle of the continent.

5
00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:56.927 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It takes its name from the rapids of the Ubangi at its edge, and the French
founded it as a post in 1889; the country sits at the hub of the Congo Basin,
with the rivers running outward in every direction.

6
00:00:57.589 --> 00:01:03.510 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the river post at the top of the Congo Basin, where the continent
meets itself.

7
00:01:04.214 --> 00:01:06.339 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Brazzaville into view.

8
00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:20.230 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Brazzaville stands on the right bank of the Congo River, in the Republic of
the Congo, west of the larger Congo across the water.

9
00:01:20.894 --> 00:01:38.498 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Basilica of Saint Anne, of 1949, is roofed in green tiles, and across the
river Kinshasa is closer to it than any other two capitals in the world are to
each other, apart from Rome and the Vatican.

10
00:01:39.164 --> 00:01:47.697 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Brazzaville on the right bank and Kinshasa on the left: the closest
pair of capitals on earth, across one river.

11
00:01:50.301 --> 00:02:00.367 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kinshasa stands on the north bank of the Congo River, directly opposite
Brazzaville, in the far larger Democratic Republic of the Congo.

12
00:02:01.031 --> 00:02:10.331 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was called Léopoldville until 1966, and with well over ten million people
it is one of the largest French-speaking cities in the world.

13
00:02:10.991 --> 00:02:18.027 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital named after the country’s own river, on the north bank
opposite Brazzaville.

14
00:02:18.716 --> 00:02:21.096 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Luanda into view.

15
00:02:26.364 --> 00:02:32.970 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Luanda stands on the Atlantic coast of Angola, north of Namibia and south of
the Congo basin.

16
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:47.656 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The fortress of São Miguel, of 1576, looks down over the harbour, which was
the main embarkation point for enslaved people carried to the Americas.

17
00:02:48.318 --> 00:02:57.188 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Atlantic harbour founded in the sixteenth century, with the fort
above it and the ships that left from below.

18
00:02:57.874 --> 00:03:00.300 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Lilongwe into view.

19
00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:11.749 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lilongwe stands in the interior of Malawi, a narrow inland country between
Zambia and Mozambique.

20
00:03:12.407 --> 00:03:27.721 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was planned as a garden city in the 1970s and took over from Zomba, the old
colonial capital; Four Freedoms Park nearby marks where Malawi’s independence
flag was raised in 1964.

21
00:03:28.382 --> 00:03:34.477 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the planned inland capital of Malawi, with the independence flag-pole
park.

22
00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:38.714 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Lusaka into view.

23
00:03:42.112 --> 00:03:48.080 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lusaka stands in the middle of Zambia, north of the Zambezi and west of
Malawi.

24
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:59.054 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It became the capital of the British protectorate of Northern Rhodesia in
1935, and the capital of independent Zambia in 1964.

25
00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:07.731 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital in the middle of Zambia, north of the falls and far from
any coast.

26
00:04:08.418 --> 00:04:10.543 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Harare into view.

27
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:21.562 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Harare stands on the highveld of Zimbabwe, north of South Africa and west of
Mozambique.

28
00:04:22.217 --> 00:04:34.071 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was called Salisbury until 1982 and then took the name of a Shona chief;
its avenues are lined with jacarandas that turn the city purple each October.

29
00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:42.617 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the renamed highveld capital of jacaranda trees, with the stone city
of Great Zimbabwe to its south-west.

30
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.425 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Maputo into view.

31
00:04:50.019 --> 00:04:59.446 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Maputo stands on Maputo Bay on the Indian Ocean, in the south of Mozambique,
where the country curves around the southern end of Africa.

32
00:05:00.098 --> 00:05:12.637 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was called Lourenço Marques until 1976, and its railway station of 1910 is
often wrongly credited to Gustave Eiffel.

33
00:05:13.303 --> 00:05:20.292 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the station on Maputo Bay that is often wrongly credited to Eiffel,
on the Indian Ocean side.

34
00:05:25.955 --> 00:05:34.524 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bangui stands at the top of the Congo Basin, Brazzaville on the right bank of
the Congo River and Kinshasa opposite it on the left.

35
00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:53.405 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Portuguese harbour at Luanda with its fort above, the planned
inland capital at Lilongwe, the middle of Zambia at Lusaka, the renamed
highveld capital at Harare, and the natural harbour with the only natural
lighthouse in the world at Maputo.
